WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.640
Your backend servers are fast.

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:05.356
A typical request takes ten milliseconds.

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:10.114
But about one request in a hundred hits a hiccup, and takes a full second.

00:00:10.364 --> 00:00:13.986
One slow request in a hundred sounds rare enough to live with.

00:00:14.236 --> 00:00:20.610
Now a user's page calls a hundred of these servers at once, and waits until every one has answered.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.648
How often is the page slow?

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:29.622
The page is fast only if all hundred calls are fast.

00:00:29.872 --> 00:00:33.151
Each call is fast ninety-nine times out of a hundred.

00:00:33.201 --> 00:00:39.341
Assume the hiccups are independent: one server hitting a hiccup doesn't make another more likely to.

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:47.137
Then the hundred calls are like a hundred separate coin flips, each coin weighted to come up fast ninety-nine times in a hundred.

00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:54.410
The chance that all hundred come up fast is ninety-nine percent, times ninety-nine percent, a hundred times over.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.033
That comes to about thirty-seven percent.

00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:00.733
So sixty-three percent of pages wait at least a second.

00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:04.749
For one server, a slow call was the rare case.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:06.959
For the page, it's the usual case.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:11.811
A dashboard of each server's average response time wouldn't warn you.

00:01:11.861 --> 00:01:19.152
Ninety-nine calls at ten milliseconds and one at a second average out to about twenty milliseconds, which looks healthy.

00:01:19.402 --> 00:01:21.865
The slow calls show up in the percentiles.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:27.158
The ninety-ninth percentile is the time that ninety-nine percent of calls finish within.

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:29.496
For these servers, it's about a second.

00:01:29.746 --> 00:01:34.868
Those rare, slow calls make up the long tail of each server's latency distribution.

00:01:34.918 --> 00:01:37.211
That's why this is called tail latency.

00:01:37.461 --> 00:01:42.825
Most pages that wait on a hundred servers include a call from their slowest one percent.

00:01:42.875 --> 00:01:49.987
So with a hundred calls, the typical page is as slow as the servers' ninety-ninth percentile, not their average.

00:01:50.037 --> 00:01:54.664
With more calls per page, an even rarer, slower percentile decides.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.157
Turn that around.

00:01:56.207 --> 00:02:04.827
For the page itself to be slow only one time in a hundred, each of its hundred servers would have to be slow only one time in ten thousand.

00:02:06.777 --> 00:02:09.519
Why not just find the slow one percent and fix it?

00:02:09.769 --> 00:02:12.842
Because hiccups have many causes, and most are brief.

00:02:12.892 --> 00:02:14.918
A burst of traffic builds a queue.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:17.486
A garbage collector pauses the program.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:23.952
A background job, or another program on the same machine, borrows the disk or the processor for a moment.

00:02:24.202 --> 00:02:28.128
Each one is short, and lands on whichever calls happen to be running.

00:02:28.178 --> 00:02:34.387
You can remove some causes and make hiccups rarer, but in a large system you can't make them all go away.

00:02:34.637 --> 00:02:39.880
So large systems are built to tolerate them, the way they tolerate machines that fail.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.472
One standard technique is the hedged request.

00:02:43.722 --> 00:02:45.427
Send the call to one server.

00:02:45.477 --> 00:02:55.685
If it hasn't answered by the ninety-fifth percentile, the time that ninety-five percent of calls finish within, send a copy to a second server that holds the same data.

00:02:55.735 --> 00:02:58.204
Use whichever answer comes back first.

00:02:58.454 --> 00:03:05.536
A call that hit a hiccup is usually rescued by the copy, as long as the two servers don't hit a hiccup at the same moment.

00:03:05.586 --> 00:03:11.279
So the copy goes to a server on a different machine, where the same hiccup is unlikely to reach it.

00:03:11.529 --> 00:03:12.803
The cost is small.

00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:19.818
Only calls slower than the ninety-fifth percentile get a copy, so the servers see about five percent more requests.

00:03:20.068 --> 00:03:26.499
In our simulation, hedging cut the share of slow pages from sixty-three percent to about one percent.

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:30.027
Google measured hedging on a real system.

00:03:30.077 --> 00:03:34.922
In one benchmark, each request read a thousand values from a hundred servers.

00:03:34.972 --> 00:03:49.943
Across many of these requests, hedging after a fixed ten milliseconds cut the 99.9th percentile, the time that 99.9 percent of them finish within, from 1.8 seconds to 74 milliseconds.

00:03:49.993 --> 00:03:52.444
It cost only two percent more requests.

00:03:53.894 --> 00:03:56.905
But hedge too early, and every call gets a copy.

00:03:56.955 --> 00:04:03.131
That's twice the load, and more load means longer queues, so busier servers answer more slowly.

00:04:03.181 --> 00:04:07.169
Waiting for the ninety-fifth percentile keeps the extra load small.

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:08.862
Hedging suits reads.

00:04:08.912 --> 00:04:14.597
A copy of a write, like a payment, could happen twice, and making that safe is the next lesson.

00:04:15.547 --> 00:04:25.976
So the page was slow sixty-three percent of the time because it waited on a hundred servers, and a one-in-a-hundred hiccup is more likely than not to hit at least one of them.

00:04:26.226 --> 00:04:32.871
When a request fans out, watch your servers' ninety-ninth percentile, their tail latency, not their average.

00:04:32.921 --> 00:04:37.087
And instead of hunting every hiccup, design the fan-out to tolerate them.

00:04:37.337 --> 00:04:40.090
All of this assumes the hiccups are independent.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:47.873
When one cause hits many servers at once, like a network blip, the arithmetic changes, and a hedge's copy may be slow too.
